::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Source: Speakers use transitions to show how ideas connect. Which paraphrase is most accurate?
{
~A. Transitions make speeches confusing.#This reverses the source meaning. The source says transitions show connections.
=B. Speakers use transitions to help listeners understand relationships between ideas.#Correct. This keeps the source meaning and changes the wording.
~C. Speakers should avoid connecting ideas.#This contradicts the source.
~D. Transitions are unrelated to speeches.#This is inaccurate because the source directly connects transitions to speaking.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.03
Question:
Source: Eye contact can make listeners feel included in a presentation. What is the key meaning?
{
~A. Eye contact prevents all speaking mistakes.#This adds a claim the source does not make.
~B. Presentations do not need listeners.#This contradicts the communication purpose.
~C. Eye contact should be avoided during presentations.#This reverses the source idea.
=D. Looking at listeners can help them feel part of the speech.#Correct. This restates the source meaning accurately.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Which paraphrase problem occurs when a student changes the source meaning?
{
=A. Inaccuracy#Correct. A paraphrase must keep the source meaning accurate.
~B. Strong academic vocabulary#Academic vocabulary can support accuracy when used correctly.
~C. Clear speech notes#Clear notes are helpful, not a paraphrase problem.
~D. Audience awareness#Audience awareness is important but does not describe changing meaning.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which word is the most precise academic vocabulary for a speech lesson?
{
~A. stuff#This word is vague and informal.
~B. things#This word is too general for precise speech notes.
=C. transition#Correct. Transition is a precise communication term.
~D. whatever#This is informal and not academically precise.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Source: Practice helps speakers find unclear parts of a presentation. Which paraphrase is best?
{
=A. Rehearsing can help speakers notice which parts of a presentation need clarification.#Correct. This restates the meaning with new wording.
~B. Practice makes every presentation perfect.#This exaggerates the source and changes the meaning.
~C. Speakers should never revise unclear parts.#This contradicts the source.
~D. Presentations are unclear because practice is impossible.#This is unrelated and inaccurate.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.03
Question:
What should a speaker do before paraphrasing a complex sentence?
{
~A. Copy the sentence exactly.#Copying does not show understanding or paraphrasing.
=B. Identify the source meaning.#Correct. Understanding the meaning is the first step before restating it.
~C. Remove all academic words.#Some academic words may be necessary for accuracy.
~D. Add a personal opinion.#Adding an opinion can change the meaning.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Which choice is closest to copying rather than paraphrasing?
{
~A. A new sentence that keeps the meaning.#This describes a valid paraphrase.
~B. A short speech note in the speaker's own words.#This can be useful if it stays accurate.
=C. The original sentence with only one or two words changed.#Correct. This keeps too much of the source wording and structure.
~D. A restatement that changes the sentence pattern.#Changing structure is part of paraphrasing.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Source: Note cards can keep a speaker from losing the order of ideas. Which paraphrase is accurate?
{
~A. Note cards cause speakers to forget ideas.#This reverses the source meaning.
~B. Speakers should write every word on note cards.#This adds a claim that is not in the source.
~C. Ideas do not need order during a speech.#This contradicts the source.
=D. Note cards can help speakers remember the sequence of their ideas.#Correct. This keeps the meaning and uses new wording.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Why might a paraphrase keep the word audience?
{
=A. It is a precise communication term needed for meaning.#Correct. Some academic terms should stay because they name important concepts.
~B. It makes the paraphrase longer for no reason.#Length is not the purpose of keeping academic vocabulary.
~C. It hides the source meaning.#Precise vocabulary should clarify meaning, not hide it.
~D. It makes the paraphrase informal.#Audience is an appropriate academic communication word.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Source: A summary includes only the most important ideas from a longer text. Which statement is true?
{
~A. A summary must include every sentence from the source.#A summary is shorter and includes only the most important ideas.
~B. A summary should change the source meaning.#A summary must stay accurate.
~C. A summary is always longer than the source.#A summary is usually shorter than the source.
=D. A summary is shorter and focuses on the main ideas.#Correct. This describes the difference between summary and full source text.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.03
Question:
Source: Speakers should check whether their evidence clearly supports the point being made. What is the meaning?
{
~A. Evidence should be unrelated to the point.#This reverses the source meaning.
=B. Speakers need to make sure evidence connects to the point.#Correct. This accurately restates the meaning.
~C. Points do not need evidence.#This contradicts the source.
~D. Evidence is only for written work.#The source applies evidence to speaking.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Which paraphrase changes the meaning of this source: Clear notes help speakers deliver information smoothly.
{
~A. Organized notes can help a speaker present information more smoothly.#This keeps the original meaning.
~B. Speakers may deliver information more smoothly when their notes are clear.#This accurately restates the source.
=C. Clear notes make speaking unnecessary.#Correct. This changes the meaning because notes support speaking; they do not replace it.
~D. Notes that are easy to follow can support smooth delivery.#This is an accurate paraphrase.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
What is the best speech note from this source: A speaker should pause briefly after a major point so the audience can process the idea.
{
=A. Pause after major point; audience needs time to process.#Correct. This is concise and keeps the source meaning for speaking.
~B. A speaker should pause briefly after a major point so the audience can process the idea.#This copies the source instead of making a note.
~C. Never pause during a speech.#This changes the source meaning.
~D. Audience processing does not matter.#This contradicts the source.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which phrase uses academic vocabulary most appropriately?
{
~A. Talk thing#This is vague and informal.
~B. Speech stuff#This does not use precise academic language.
~C. Some speaking things#This is vague and not useful for notes.
=D. Presentation structure#Correct. This phrase is precise and appropriate for speech notes.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Source: An effective conclusion reminds the audience of the speech's central idea. Which paraphrase is best?
{
=A. A strong closing brings listeners back to the main focus of the speech.#Correct. This keeps the meaning with new wording.
~B. Conclusions should introduce an unrelated topic.#This contradicts the source.
~C. The audience should forget the central idea.#This reverses the source meaning.
~D. A conclusion is only a list of random facts.#This is inaccurate and unfocused.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.03
Question:
When reading a source for speech notes, what should the speaker identify?
{
~A. Only the longest word.#The longest word may not be the most important idea.
=B. The important idea and supporting meaning.#Correct. These are needed for accurate paraphrasing.
~C. A new opinion to add.#Adding opinion can change the source meaning.
~D. A way to avoid the source meaning.#Avoiding meaning prevents accurate paraphrasing.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Which choice best describes a paraphrase?
{
~A. A copied sentence with quotation marks removed.#This is copying, not paraphrasing.
~B. A personal opinion about the source.#A paraphrase restates the source, not the speaker's opinion.
=C. A restatement of source information in new wording that keeps the meaning.#Correct. This defines accurate paraphrasing.
~D. A sentence that removes the main idea.#Removing the main idea makes the paraphrase inaccurate.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Source: Visual aids can support a speech when they clarify information for the audience. Which paraphrase is accurate?
{
=A. Visual aids are helpful when they make speech information clearer for listeners.#Correct. This keeps the meaning and changes the wording.
~B. Visual aids always replace speaking.#This adds an incorrect claim.
~C. Audiences should ignore visual aids.#This contradicts the source.
~D. Visual aids are never connected to information.#This reverses the source meaning.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which word pair is most useful for academic speech notes?
{
~A. stuff and things#These are vague words.
~B. whatever and maybe#These are informal and imprecise.
=C. focus and evidence#Correct. These are precise academic communication words.
~D. cool and random#These are informal and not specific enough.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
What should a speaker do after writing a paraphrase?
{
~A. Never compare it to the source.#Comparison helps check accuracy.
~B. Assume it is correct because it is shorter.#Shorter does not always mean accurate.
~C. Add unrelated facts to make it longer.#Unrelated facts change the meaning and weaken notes.
=D. Compare it with the source to check meaning.#Correct. Checking helps ensure the paraphrase is accurate.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.03
Question:
Source: Complex texts may require rereading before a speaker can explain them clearly. What does this mean?
{
=A. A speaker may need to read a difficult source more than once to understand it.#Correct. This captures the source meaning.
~B. Speakers should avoid all complex texts.#The source says rereading may help, not that complex texts should be avoided.
~C. Rereading makes explanation impossible.#This contradicts the source.
~D. Clear explanation does not require understanding.#Understanding is necessary for clear explanation.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Which paraphrase is too close to the source: Speakers can use brief notes to avoid reading every word.
{
~A. Short notes can help speakers avoid reading word for word.#This is accurate but somewhat close; it changes enough to be a paraphrase.
=B. Speakers can use brief notes to avoid reading every word.#Correct. This copies the source exactly.
~C. Brief speaker notes can support natural delivery.#This restates the idea more generally.
~D. Notes with only key ideas can keep a speaker from reading the whole speech.#This changes wording and structure while keeping meaning.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Why are paraphrased notes useful in speeches?
{
~A. They allow speakers to ignore source meaning.#Ignoring meaning leads to inaccurate communication.
~B. They make speakers read directly from the source.#Paraphrased notes should support natural explanation.
=C. They help speakers explain source ideas in their own words.#Correct. This is the purpose of paraphrased speech notes.
~D. They remove all academic vocabulary.#Useful notes may keep needed academic words.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which revision makes a speech note more precise?
{
~A. Change "audience response" to "stuff."#This makes the note less precise.
~B. Change "evidence" to "thing."#This removes useful academic vocabulary.
~C. Change "transition" to "whatever comes next."#This is informal and less precise.
=D. Change "talk part" to "main point."#Correct. Main point is precise academic speech vocabulary.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L03_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L03_QZ_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Which statement best summarizes this lesson?
{
=A. A speaker should understand source meaning and restate it accurately in their own words for speech notes.#Correct. This captures the lesson focus on accurate paraphrasing.
~B. A speaker should copy every source sentence into the speech.#Copying does not show paraphrase or natural speech preparation.
~C. A speaker should change the meaning to make the speech more exciting.#Changing meaning makes the paraphrase inaccurate.
~D. A speaker should avoid all academic vocabulary.#Precise academic vocabulary can be useful when it is accurate.
}
